The simplest way to use Gamma is by invoking it on the command line,
like you would a compiler^[In fact the command line interface was
designed to resemble that of the GNU Compiler Collection.].  Consider
the following simple program:

Document: manual/scheme
```scheme
(import (gamma polyhedra) (gamma operations))

(set-curve-tolerance! 1/1000)

(define-output cube
  (intersection (cuboid 1 1 1) (sphere 7/10)))
```
Document: manual/lua
```lua
local polyhedra = require 'gamma.polyhedra'
local operations = require 'gamma.operations'

set_curve_tolerance(0.001)

output(
   "cube",
   operations.intersection(
      polyhedra.cuboid(1, 1, 1),
      polyhedra.sphere(0.7)))
```
Document: manual/all

Place it in a file called f`cube.ext` in the current directory, then
run Gamma with s`gamma -o cube.stl cube.ext`.  It will produce a model
of a cube with rounded edges in STL format, as a file named
f`cube.stl`. If you open it in a model viewer, you should see the
geometry shown in ref:rounded-cube.

Figure: rounded-cube
```print
# split horizontally
# focus 2
# rotate 51 24 26
# view 30

(import (gamma polyhedra) (gamma operations))
(set-curve-tolerance! 1/1000)

#>1 (intersection (cuboid 1 1 1) (sphere 7/10))
#>2 (intersection (cuboid 1 1 1) (sphere 7/10))
```
  The result produced by a simple program taking the intersection of a
  sphere and a cube.

This is similar to programming in a compiled language and many of the
tecniques used with such languages apply here as well.  For instance,
you can place the invocation shown above in a Makefile, so that you
only need to run c`make` to produce the result.

Concept: debugger

If you prefer a graphical user interface, you can use Gamma Debugger,
henceforth referred to as the def:Debugger.  It can invoke Gamma for
you and display the resulting geometry in one or more windows, where
you can inspect it interactively.  This is convenient when writing a
program, where you would typically want to make changes to it and
inspect the results repeat until the desired output is achieved.

The simplest way to use the Debugger with the program contained in
f`cube.ext` is by invoking it as shown below.

```
gammadb --target cube --args cube.ext
```

This will start the debugger, which will print some introductory
messages, followed by the prompt s`#`.  The o`--target` and o`--args`
options provide the minimal configuration needed to run the program
and display the single output contained in it and will be fully
explained later.

You can now type the command s`run` at the prompt to invoke Gamma to
compile the model.  A window should appear with the geometry shown in
ref: rounded-cube. You can use the mouse to rotate, translate, or zoom
the view, by dragging the cursor with the left, right and middle mouse
buttons respectively.

If you were to decide that the cube isn't rounded quite enough, you
could simply edit f`cube.ext`, change the sphere radius from s`7/10`
to s`2/3` and repeat the run command^[You can do this by simply
pressing return, which is equivalent to entering the last command
again.].  The displayed model should change accordingly.

When you've finished working, enter the command c`exit`, or c`quit`,
or press k`Ctrl-D` to exit the Debugger.

Unalias: .ext

## A Program in Broad Strokes

Although very simple, the program shown in the previous section
illustrates the basic structure of most programs.  After importing
functions from one or more libraries, they are first used to create
def:{primitive geometry}, that is, geometry that isn't based on some
previous result, but is produced from scratch.  These are typically
simple geometric objects and are therefore rarely the intended output
of the program. Instead, they serve as basic building blocks which are
combined and further processed by functions that operate on them.

Concept: operations

Here sphere and cube primitives are produced using the `sphere` and
`cuboid` functions imported from the `polyhedra` library and then
intersected using the `intersection` function imported from the
`operations` library.  By carefully chosing the sphere radius^[The
value s`7/10` is close to $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$, so that the sphere
barely extends to the midpoints of the cube's faces.  Try changing
s`intersection` to s`union` and rerunning the program for an
illustration.]  most of the cube is inside the sphere; only its
corners extend out of it and are rounded off by the intersection.

Concept: outputs

Although the program calls three functions that produce geometry, not
all results are of immediate interest.  The sphere and cube geometry
are only needed to take their intersection to produce the rounded
cube, which is the intended result of a program.  This result^[More
correctly, the operation which will produce the result.  When a
function such as c`sphere` is called, it does not immediately return
the geometry of a sphere.  Instead it returns an def:operation, which
will be evaluated in a subsequent stage to produce the desired
outputs.  For more details, ref: Running Programs.] is therefore
designated as an def:output.  There can be more than one outputs and
each can be saved to disk or sent to the Debugger for inspection.

## A Few Examples

The following chapters describe all of Gamma's features in detail, but
before doing that, it might be helpful to give a few examples of
practical programs.  If you're new to Gamma, don't worry if you don't
understand them in detail.  Their purpose is to give you an idea of
how Gamma is used in practice and what you'll be able to do with it.
They will also serve as a more concrete basis for the introduction of
some of the fundamental concepts and techniques involved.

It is still recommended that you try to follow along by copying the
code and trying it out.  If you want to do so, you should create a
directory dedicated to the purpose, as you would for any program.
Besides keeping things organized, it will avoid flooding your home
directory (or the directory from which you would run Gamma) with a
multitude of files that Gamma often creates during compilation.

