Inside this directory, place a file named f`.gammadbinit` with the
following contents:

```init
# Set up the window.

window examples
resize 1500 1000
split horizontally 3 1
split vertically 2
focus 2
target sprocket

# Bind some keys to commands.

bind r run single
bind R run all
bind q quit

# Set the compilation command and run it.

set args --dump-list=- *.ext
define draft
run
```

This file is called an def:{initialization file} and when the Debugger
starts, it will look for it in the current directory, read it and
execute any commands it finds inside.^[Lines starting with a s`#` are
comments and are ignored.  So are blank lines.]

This illustrates the most practical way to use the Debugger in
practice. Once you've set up the initialization file, you only need to
run s`gammadb` on the terminal to start it.

The commands in the init file shown above will:

  1. Create a window and divide it into three viewports.  You can have
  as many windows as you need and each can be split arbitrarily into
  def:viewports.  These are separate sections of the window, each
  dedicated to showing geometry generated by the output whose name
  matches its target.

  2. Set up some basic key bindings so that you don't have to run
  commands in the terminal.  Pressing the k`r` key while the mouse is
  over a viewport^[The viewport is then highlighted and is said to be
  def:focused.] will run the needed compilation command to rebuild and
  reload the output displayed in it.  Conversely, pressing k`R`, which
  is to say k`Shift-r`, will rebuild and reload all viewports.
  Finally, pressing k`q` will quit the Debugger.

  3. Set up the command line that will be used to run Gamma, so that
  it will execute all files ending in f`.ext` and print out each
  operation of the computation as it evaluates it.  The latter can be
  useful, to give you an idea of where and how Gamma spends its time.
  The s`define draft` command also affects the compilation command and
  its purpose will be made clear shortly.

  4. Run Gamma as required to refresh all viewports.  Windows normally
  start out hidden to keep them of the way until they have something
  to show.  Once some geometry is loaded for one or more of their
  viewports, they're presented to the user.  Here, this will happen
  when the final c`run` command is executed.

