Concept: outputs

## Program Outputs

The final result is the union of these components, extruded to the
specified width. This would normally be followed by further processing
to give the sprocket a proper rim profile as the specification
demands, but this is not done here so as not to further complicate the
example.

Since this is the end result of the program, it is designated as an
ouput.  This makes it possible to produce a spocket of any
configuration and save it to a file in one of the supported formats.
For example, the following invocation will produce a sprocket with 9
teeth, matching a chain with 4mm diameter rollers and a pitch of 6mm.

```
gamma -Dz=9 -Dd_1=4 -Dp=6 -o sprocket.stl sprocket.scm
```

It will be written in STL format, to the file f`sprocket.stl` in the
current directory.

Outputs can also be inspected in the Debugger.  When the c`run`
command is run, the Debugger will invoke Gamma with the necessary
o`-o` options to enable outputs for each of the current window's
viewport targets.  If matching outputs are defined in the program,
their geometry is loaded in the corresponding viewports.  Since the
window is divided into three viewports, two of which have their
default targets, s`1` and s`2`, while the third's target is
s`sprocket`, these are the outputs that will be loaded on each run.

The outputs s`1` and s`2` are meant for debugging.  In this example,
they are set to the two components that make up the sprocket's
cross-section, but in general you would set them to output the part of
the design you're currently working on.  The `output` function returns
its first argument, so you can usually just wrap any expression that
returns geometry that you'd like to inspect inside an `output` call
and press the k`r` key on the respective viewport.  This is
essentially analogous to ``@w{}`printf()` debugging''.

