## A Threaded Fastener

Concept: extrusion

The previous example involved a trivial extrusion of a complex polygon
assembled from boolean operations on simple shapes.  This example
illustrates the flexibility of the extrusion operation by doing the
reverse.  A simple shape, the basic ISO metric screw thread profile,
is extruded along a helical path to form the threads of a bolt.  To
that is added a cylinder to complete the shaft and a chamfered hex
head.

Document: manual/scheme
```scheme bolt.scm
```
Document: manual/lua
```lua bolt.lua
```
Document: manual/all

The extrusion operation takes a polygon and a list of arbitrary
transformations.  It transforms the polygon with each of the
transformations in order and connects the corresponding vertices to
form a solid.  Here, each copy of the thread profile polygon is first
translated `r` units away from the central axis of the bolt and also
progressively further along the axis, based on the pitch `P`.  To that
is added a rotation around the axis, so that the composition of these
transformations forms a helix along which the thread profile is
extruded.

The final 90 degree rotation^[This rotation appears first in the code.
That is because a concatenation of three transformations $A$, $B$, $C$
is applied to each vertex $v$ of a polygon or polyhedron as $A \cdot B
\cdot C \cdot v$, so that the order of application is reversed.]
merely serves to rotate the threads as a whole and could just as well
have been applied to the result of the extrusion operation.  To
illustrate that any transformation can be used in the extrusion, a
gradual scaling is included in the first revolution.  This tapers the
thread towards the end of the bolt, to ease threading into a nut.

Figure: bolt
```print bolt.scm
# set print-frames yes
# split horizontally 3 1
# split vertically 2
# focus 2
# target bolt
# rotate 55 -40 -20
# zoom -0.2
# focus 3
# zoom -0.5
# rotate 90 -45 30
```
  The output of the fastener example program, as seen in the Debugger.
  The lower left viewport shows the thread profile polygon, which is
  extruded along a helical path, forming the threads shown in the
  upper left viewport.  A shaft and a chamfered hexagonal head is
  added, to form the final bolt shown in the right viewport.

The `draft_p` parameter is used here to select the number of steps for
each revolution of the thread, but its purpose and effect is the same
as before.  Also, here too you can vary the diameter `d`, pitch `P`
and length `L`, to get any metric thread, or vary the profile polygon
to get other kinds of threads.  Obtaining a nut, is just a matter of
taking a difference with the hexagonal head instead of a union.

## Faired Surfaces

Concept: surface fairing

The previous examples dealt with solids produced by the composition of
simple shapes, either linear or made up of simple circular curves.  A
final example demonstrates that more complex surfaces can also be
obtained, often without much effort.

The following program produces a ``Wye'' fitting, such as one might use
to connect three pipes, splitting a flow into two branches, each at 45
degrees to the incoming direction.

Document: manual/scheme
```scheme fitting.scm
```
Document: manual/lua
```lua fitting.lua
```
Document: manual/all

The final part is produced in three steps.  First a basic,
``unfaired'' version of the part is produced by distributing the three
cylindrical ends at the required angles and at a certain distance from
the center of the mesh given by the parameter `radius`.  Here too,
this is accomplished by means of a placement function that transforms
the ends into place.  To that is added the simplest possible geometry
that can join the ends together: the convex hull of their bases.

The ends are formed by extrusions of the circular shapes that define
their cross-sections to a length given by the parameter `height`.  The
bases that must be hulled together to form the central section are
formed from the same shapes, extruded to zero length^[By convention,
such a degenerate extrusion creates a three dimensional version of the
poygons it operates on.].

This process yields a solid version of the fitting. The hollow part is
produced by subtracting from it a second version, where the
cross-sections have been reduced by the desired wall width, given by
the parameter `width`.

The result can be seen in the lower left viewport of ref: wye-fitting.
It is functional as is, but not very pleasant aesthetically^[The sharp
edges would also likely disturb the flow of liquids through it,
depending on the parameters of the flow.].  While the ends must be
kept unaltered, as they need to mate with the incoming and outgoing
pipes, the central section can be made more smooth.

This is accomplished with the `fair` operation, which moves the
vertices of a mesh so as to make it ``smoother'', but since the base
mesh has no vertices in the area that must be smoothed, applying
fairing to it as it is would not have much effect.  It must first be
refined using the `remesh` operation.

Figure: wye-fitting
```print fitting.scm
# set print-frames yes
# split horizontally 3 1
# split vertically 2
# rotate 0 -90
# focus 2
# rotate 0 -60 30
# target fitting
# focus 3
# rotate 0 -90
```
  The output of the fitting example program, as seen in the Debugger.
  The lower left viewport shows the basic mesh, produced by taking the
  union of the ends with the convex hull of their bases.  The remeshed
  version of this is shown in the upper left viewport, while the final
  faired version is shown in the right viewport.

The remeshing operation splits, flips and moves the edges and vertices
of the input, so as to produce an ``isotropic'' mesh, that is, one
whose edges are all close to a given target length, keeping the shape
as much as possible the same.  When operating on geometry with
features that must be exactly preserved, it can often still distort
the shape to an unacceptable degree.  Had it been applied without any
constraints to the base mesh of the fitting, such distortions would be
visible in the sharp edges of the ends, as can be seen in ref:
more-fittings.

Concept: selections

To avoid this problem, the remeshing operation optionally accepts a
set of edges that should be protected, in the from of a def:selection.
Such selections can be made on the vertex, edge, or face level and can
be obtained in various ways.  In this case edges are selected based on
their ``sharpness angle'', the angle formed by their incident faces,
and which should be 90 degrees or larger.  As a result, only the sharp
edges at the ends are selected and protected during remeshing, as can
be seen in the upper left viewport in ref:wye-fitting.

Figure: more-fittings
```print fittings.scm
# resize 750 250
# split horizontally 2
# split vertically 2
# rotate 0 -90
# zoom -0.2
# focus 2
# split vertically 2
# rotate 0 -60 -45
# zoom -0.4
# focus 3
# rotate 15 -85 120
# zoom -0.2
# focus 4
# rotate 0 -60
# zoom -0.2
```
  More outputs from the fitting example program.  From left to right,
  top to bottom: The result of remeshing the basic geometry without
  constraints, leading to distortions in the sharp edges of the ends,
  a reducing coupler, an eccentric reducer and a reducing tee.

The final step is the application of the `fair` operation.  Given a
mesh and a vertex selection, it smooths the surface patches that are
inside the selection as much as possible, subject to the constraint
that they conform to the parts that are not selected and which are
left undisturbed.  This often achieves the desired result and only
requires selection of the patches where smoothing should be
applied.^[When this is not enough, considerable control of the output
can be achieved by careful manipulation of the selection and the base
geometry at its boundaries.]

Concept: bounding volumes

In this case, fairing must only be applied to the vertices of the
central portion created by the hulling operation.  These are selected
as those lying inside the volume defined by the intersection of three
bounding halfspaces, a kind of def:{bounding volume}^[There are many
kinds of bounding volumes, such as boxes, spheres and cylinders and
they can be transformed and combined much like geometry.], placed at
the bases of the three ends by means of the placement function.  Each
bounding halfspace selects all vertices that lie below the respective
end, so that their intersection produces the desired selection.  The
result can be seen in the right viewport of ref:wye-fitting.

This approach is perhaps more complex than is needed for the design of
the Wye fitting, but it has the advatage of generality.  Three more
kinds of fittings that can be obtained by a mere change of the shapes
and angles specified in the final call of the program are shown in
ref:more-fittings.  It also illustrates the general utility of the
fairing operation, which regrettably often comes at a high
computational cost.  To deal with this, the `draft_p` parameter is
used again, here to select a coarser mesh through higher curve
tolerance and remeshing targets during development.  Try to undefine
it and run the build again.  The result should be more detailed and of
higher quality, but be prepared for a longer wait.

